An optimized and parallelized version of the program is given below.

### Optimizations
1. **Cache Blocking**: The original kernel repeatedly sweeps over the entire arrays ($2 \times 160\text{ MB} = 320\text{ MB}$), exceeding the CPU cache and incurring massive DRAM traffic (over 9 GB). By blocking the iteration space into blocks of size $B = 2048$ doubles ($16\text{ KB}$ for $x$ and $16\text{ KB}$ for $y$), each block fits entirely within the L1 data cache of both P-cores ($128\text{ KB}$) and E-cores ($64\text{ KB}$). Elements are read from DRAM once, updated $reps$ times at L1 cache speed, and written back to DRAM once.
2. **Heterogeneous Load Balancing**: The M4 chip has 4 P-cores and 6 slower E-cores. A static schedule would leave P-cores idle waiting for E-cores. We use `schedule(dynamic, 8)` so cores dynamically request chunks of blocks. P-cores naturally process more chunks than E-cores, eliminating load imbalance.
3. **Vectorization**: Adding `restrict` pointers allows Clang to verify that $x$ and $y$ do not alias, enabling auto-vectorization using ARM NEON instructions without runtime alias checks.

```c
/* k01_saxpy: y = a*x + y, repeated. UNIFORM cost per iteration, memory-bound. */
#include <omp.h>
#include "common.h"

/* === KERNEL BEGIN === */
static void kernel(long n, int reps, double a, const double *x, double *y) {
    if (n <= 0 || reps <= 0) return;

    const double * restrict rx = x;
    double * restrict ry = y;
    const long B = 2048;

    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic, 8)
    for (long ib = 0; ib < n; ib += B) {
        long i_end = ib + B < n ? ib + B : n;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; r++) {
            #pragma clang loop vectorize(enable)
            #pragma clang loop interleave(enable)
            for (long i = ib; i < i_end; i++) {
                ry[i] = a * rx[i] + ry[i];
            }
        }
    }
}
/* === KERNEL END === */

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    long n = arg_long(argc, argv, 1, 20000000);
    int reps = (int)arg_long(argc, argv, 2, 20);
    double *x = xmalloc(n * sizeof *x), *y = xmalloc(n * sizeof *y);
    rng_seed(1);
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) { x[i] = rng_unit(); y[i] = rng_unit(); }

    double t0 = now_sec();
    kernel(n, reps, 0.5, x, y);
    double t1 = now_sec();

    double sum = 0.0;
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += y[i];
    report("saxpy", t1 - t0, sum);
    free(x); free(y);
    return 0;
}
```
